>I must admit my ignorance about exactly how SPECmarks are
>defined and measured.   Are they good indication of performance.   How
>about SPECint and SPECfp?

	This is one problem with benchmarks. If you don't understand
them, and don't understand how vendors cheat (excuse me: "optimize")
on them, then you're really shooting in the dark and may as well
just go with which has the higher clock rate*.

	SPEC{int|fp} benchmarks, for example, don't touch on I/O. Most
benchmarks don't. Yet I/O is a crucial factor in workstation performance.
SPECint, for example, deals ONLY with integer performance on some classes
of application. Integer performance will tend to map to how fast the box
can run "normal" code (including its kernel, most UNIXes don't contain
floating point). SPECfp deals with floating point. If you don't do a
lot of number crunching, a machine with 10SPECint and 10000SPECfp is a
bad machine for you.

	One reason a lot of benchmarks leave I/O out is because it's
really hard to characterize. ;) But I'd suspect that a workstation
with a 9ms hard disk is going to clobber a workstation with a 50ms
hard disk because lots of operations (such as memory, in cases of
paging) map to disk I/O.

	Remember, when you read SPECmarks, you are looking at a small
slice of total system performance. Also, and I can say this now that
I no longer work for a vendor, some vendors cheat*. A classic example
is the way certain floating point benchmarks were "optimized" by
some vendors (IBM, HP, DEC, and others) using a preprocessor that
was specially tuned to rewrite the benchmark. A "compiler pre-optimizer"
that *somewhat* helps a small class of general purpose applications,
but for anything OTHER than the benchmark has been known to produce
invalid code. If that's not cheating, it's at least deceptive.

	How your machine runs for your day-to-day load is the BEST
benchmark possible. Period. Benchmarks, in all industries, are
usually as close to outright lies as can get, in my experience.
When a car company notes that thier car can go 0-50 in 4.2 seconds,
they're not telling you that it was done with a pro driver on a
car with extra wide tires that had been burned down, and the car
was running with octane booster in the fuel on a hot day.

	Buy machines based on a test-drive, just like you would
a car.

mjr.

*(yes, this is arguable. that's the point.)


