In article <ghsu.733524735@critical>, ghsu@critical.nswc.navy.mil () writes...
>   We are considering to purchase a few workstations.   Among our
>candidates are the two computers mentioned the the subject.   I am
>somewhat confused by the performance figures I found.  According to
>DEC catalog, 5000/240 performs at 32.4 SPECmarks, while according to
>HP catalog, HP 720 is 66.5 SPECmark89.    Are these two units
>different?    If they are the same, one would expect HP should easily
>be faster however you time them.    But according to a friend who ran
>his program on both computers that DEC is actually faster.   

Thats the problem with a "standard" benchmark such as SPECmarks: YOUR MILEAGE
WILL VARY.  It is not in the least unreasoanable to have the results you're
friend discovered, nor would the opossite be wrong.

Ever ask a HP sales types for a SPECchart?  DEC sales folks will be glad to
show you a SPECchart for the HP.  SPECmark is the geometric mean of several
benchmarks.  The HP performs fantasicly on ONE of them.  This wildly influences
the mean. I don't know numbers for the 720, but I think for the 750 they were
somewhere around 20 to 30 for most of the benchmarks, and 200 for the one,
resulting in a mean of over 75.  Only a SPECchart shows the whole SPEC ball
game.

Ever optimize you code?  I had a benchmark which measured raw instructions per
second by assuming that "i++" would generate one instruction.  Unoptimized, an
HP 700-something I had on loan (sorry, I forget the model) beat a DECstation
3100.  Optimized "O1" they were closer, and at "O2" they were about the same. 
At "O3" the DECstation's C complier noticed that the results of all those i++
was never used, optimized away the whole loop full of them, and produce the
+Infinite Nan for the results.  The HP didn't have "O3".

Remember that 1 SPECmark was defined on a VAX 11/780 running a particular
version of ULTRIX, and a particular version of the C compiler, etc.  Later
versions of ULTRIX and the compilers boosted the same VAX 11/780 performance to
around 1.1 to 1.2 SPECmarks.  

A benchmark of my own making showed that in varying the instruction mix, an
11/780 could run anywhere from under 3/4 to over 2 1/2 raw MIPS.

caveat emptor

