[PC or workstation?]

It's silly to pick a platform without looking at the big picture.  What
are you planning to do with the system?  Where are the best software
packages (and the most options) that allow you to use the system as you
intend?  Your question isn't "which platform is the better choice?", it's
"which platform will perform best for the tasks that I'm planning?".
Anyone who answers your question with a simple "Buy an Amiga" or "Buy
a PC" or "Buy a Sun" is fooling themselves.

A workstation excels because it generally has better performance than a
PC, but there are a lot of hidden costs associated with a workstation.
Software is, in general, VERY expense.  You can run PC software on most
workstations, albeit slowly, inside of an emulator package.

A PC has a derth of application software available at more reasonable
prices.  But is generally limitted to the speed of a 486DX2/66.  There
are PC based systems that use multiprocessing.  These are more on par
with a larger/faster system.  I've read that Compaq is offering a system
that can support 256 486 chips.

Software is the most important, difficult decision, hardware decisions should
be based on software requirements.

