The follwoing short program produces "correct" resusts on the
DECstation, VAX/VMS, and HP boxes, but fails with a SEGV on the AXP/VMS
when an attempt is made to change the value of the string literal.

-----
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    char *c = "x";
    
    c[0] = 'x'; c[1] = '\0';
    
    printf("c = \"%s\"\n", c);
    
    c[0] = 'y';
    printf("c = \"%s\"\n", c);
}
-----

According to the ANSI spec, 
   "If a program attempts to modify a string literal..., the behavior
   is undefined". (Section 3.1.4)
so, strickly speaking, I guess this can't be considered a bug.  Still,
it is a bit surprising to me that it does get detected and cause a
failure on the AXP/VMS, especially since it does not crash under
VAX/VMS.

Can someone explain excatly what is happening on the AXP/VMS?  Is
there a way to make this program crash under VAX/VMS (or other
platforms)?

Thanks,
   Dan
