In article <party.733103699@cwis>, party@cwis.unomaha.edu (Tom Ferony) writes:
> I'm an ACM system administrator and we were given a Vax 11/780. There is one
> BIG problem we don't have 3-Phase power. Does anyone out there know of a way
> to set up the 11/780 to take single phase instead of 3-phase??? Thanks.

  Nothing in the 78x requires 3-phase power. If you open the front doors and
swing out the RX01 floppy drive, you will see the "business end" of the sys-
tem's power controller. Note that all the outlets are single phase.

  You can do a number of things to get around this problem. The 78x uses 3-
phase power in order to reduce per-phase current consumption. You could tie
all 3 "hot" leads together. WARNING: if you do this, you will need to increase
the size of the neutral lead as it will be carrying *MUCH* more current than
it would in a 3-phase system (since if the phases are balanced there should
be 0 current in the neutral. However, there's slim chance of balanced phases
in a 78x). If you do this you could still use the main key switch to control
the system. I expect that the current drawn would be about 30A, probably a
little bit over. See if you can borrow a clamp-on ammeter to check. This also
means you probably want to use #8 or heavier wire in your power cord and use
suitable plugs/receptacles/circuit breaker.

  If you can't do that, you can go around with *heavy-duty* extension cords
and plug the individual 78x plugs into whatever outlets you can find. This
is *not* recommended. This will probably confuse the 78x into thinking that
some sort of power failure is going on - once you get it all plugged in and
powered up, you'll probably have to flip the "DC ON" switch on the LSI-11 to
restore sanity to the CPU.

  Neither of these operations is permissible under most electrical codes -
you should check with the owner of the space your'e using for approval first.
Remember that fire insurance does not have to pay if non-permissible wiring
is present.

  You may want to consider a MicroVAX II - it's about 10% slower but will
happily plug into a single regular wall outlet.

