	I TOTALLY AGREE!  Yet, I will continue to use pcs, although I would
like to experiment with the Alpha, Intel i860, Transputer, etc.  as a
daughterboard.   Intel, because of the number of processors sold and the
future possibility of processors sold, will continue to be a driving force
in the industry for at least another 20 years; of which the 80x86 line will
be the "cash cow" where the most investment of research $$$$s is put into.

	As far as what chips I would rather prefer to work on, the i860 comes 
to mind.    IMHO this is still the best processor for the money.  Unfort-
unately, the rest of the world has decided to stick with 2 tried and true
architechtures:   The 680x0 and the 80x86  (yes, I know that MIPS could be
included as a "leading architechture" too.)    This is sad, but the best
most programmers will be able to hope for is a processor that provides
faster speed but has a totally new microprocessor design on another part of
the chip.   For instance, imagine the 486 (apx 1,000,000 transistors)
coupled with an upgraded version of the i860 (which is also ~1m trans-
istors):  This would still only be 2meg transistors.   The current pentium
is supposed to have 3.5million, and the P6, set for annoucement in late
94, is supposed to have 8million.    The "best of both worlds" approach would
be to include a faster, more up to date desgin to be built onto a chip
with a better 80x86/680x0 core.   Right now, the 32-bit 386 architechture is
rapidly replacing the old 286/88 processors.   In fact, a lot of current
games and compilers don't even support 8088, and the day may soon come when
below 32-bit code will not run.  Considering that the 386 came out in 87(?)
and by 1995 almost everything will be 32-bit, We could expect a processor
design like this, if repeated in chip model after chip model to get major
acceptance after 8 years... Thus if instituted today, by 2001 we could have
a large acceptance in the design.   Although at the exponential speed that
things have been going, it could only take 5 or 6.


			Rodney

