I think this is a bit misleading.  I recently purchased a 486DX50 EISA
machine, SCSI controller, F1280 1M video card, 17" mag monitor, 250MB
colorado (junk) tape backup, 8MB RAM for about $5K.  (Couple of months
ago actually).  While this was a pretty fast and nice system (for a pc), it 
*certainly* was not tricked out by any means.  Actually, the keyboard and
mouse were very low quality.  (of course I did get a *free* copy of
DOS and Windows with it! :-)  And, of course, the F1280 drivers don't
really work *quite* right in Windows still... 

If you compare performance parts, you *will* pay as much as a "workstation".  

I for one, would much rather a SPARCstation LX or such on my desk.  I think
they key here is the workstation usually works when you put it all together.

As a previous poster indicated, support is an issue.  Typically PC support
is nearly non-existant.  Also, as a previous poster sugested, software 
really should be your driving force.  I tend to disagree with darth vader
here, and submit that the PC has *lots* of low end solutions and the 
workstation market has high end solutions.  As an example, Oracle System 7
will not be ported to DOS.  If you want some serious databases, distributed
computing, performance, etc - workstations offer a better environment.
Actually though, I belive the original poster was interested in a 
development environment.  (silly me to bring up the original post!)  I
think one of the nice things about the Unix environment is the fact you
are not hooked into one thing or another.  Do you want GUI or CLI?  Or
run lots of CLI's in your GUI (yes please).  If you run MS-windows for
a GUI / CLI environment, it's a kludge at best.  Heck, if I have to tie
up my entire system to format a floppy just *once* more... I'll...  I'll
...  I'll....  Ahem.  Wait patiently.  

Some might say, well, those are just limitations of MS-DOS & Windows.
Well, if you run Unix on it, you just get back to the system performance
and design issues.  

A PC is hobbled together, a workstation is engineered. 
 
You get what you pay for.

