> 
>  Good Morning Everyone....
> 
> 
>      While performing a batch backup, I received the error message
>      listed below. I searched through the 'gray wall' for an
>      explanation to no avail. Can anyone either explain the error or
>      direct me to the proper manual.
> 
> 	-RMS-E-EXT, ACP file extend failed
> 	-SYSTEM-F-EXQUOTA, exceeded quota
> 
>      I know this is probably basic to most, so any 'un-flamed' help
>      will be greatly appreciated. The offending backup command is
>      listed below.
> 
> 	Backup/Log/list=Carb$Dua1:[BACKUP.LOG]'Tname'.ARK/Since='Bckdate' -
>            'File' MUA0:'Tname'.BCK/Save/Rewind
> 
> 	'Tname' is the tape label.
> 	'Bckdate' is the date extracted from a data file.

    Very simply, you've  exceeded  your  disk  quota.  The first message
    tells  you  that some unspecified file couldn't be "extended",  that
    is,  written  to,  and  the  second  tells  you  why  (loosely):  an
    unspecified  quota  was  was exceeded.  Experience (and someplace in
    the docs) tells that the  quota  exceeded for a "file extend failed"
    error is _always_ your disk quota.

    The  file  that  pushed  you  over   the  top  is  almost  certainly
    "Carb$Dua1:[BACKUP.LOG]'Tname'.ARK"  which  is being written  during
    the BACKUP processing via the /LIST qualifier.  So  clean  out  that
    directory, or get your disk quota bumped up, or...

		-Ken
