In <1or2i8INNm98@sol.tis.com> mjr@tis.com (Marcus J Ranum) writes:

>>I am persuading my advisor to buy a workstation. Money is
>>not the concern, but, of course, he wants a better buy.
>>Another guy in my lab strongly suggests to buy a 486PC. 
>>So I need detailed info to convince him on buying a
>>workstation.

>	What's the difference? A 486 with a large display and a mouse,
>running BSDI or Mach or 386BSD *IS* a workstation, and a 50Mhz 486
>with 16Mb of RAM is a mighty snappy machine, and costs less than a
>disk drive for a "workstation" from a UNIX vendor. Plus, you can
>have a DOS partition and boot off a floppy into that environment,
>if you're like me and you're sick of paying huge bucks for terrible
>software for UNIX, when you can pay low prices for similar packages
>that run under DOS or Windows.

 It kind of depends.  We have several 486DX50's here running SCO Unix.  As
well as several DECStation's.

 I prefer the workstations.

 1. The RISC processor is faster.
 2. The Hardware is more reliable
 3. The workstation is easier to configure.  No stupid CMOS settings that
are meaningless to even an expert.
 4. The harddrive and network access are noticeably faster.
 5. The machine can handle a greater number of users/processes without
getting sluggish.

 Points in favor of PC based workstation:
 1. Faster character output.
 2. The software isn't on the network, so it loads faster
 3. You can run DOS stuff

 Each has certain advantages.  But I feel the major advantage of the
workstation over the PC is in quality.  If you're going to buy a PC to act
as a Unix workstation, you need quality.  Buy something from IBM or Compaq. 
One of the big name vendors that do their own engineering.

 We've had a good deal of downtime with these cheap PC's, whereas we've
never had a failure yet on the DECstation's.

 All totaled tho, I think you might find that a quality PC based Unix
workstation is going to cost you similarly to a workstation.

 For what we paid for our 486DX50's you can now buy a Sun Classic which is
better equipped in many ways.  Around $4000-5000.

 Again, you really have to consider what you're going to do.  If the
software you need to use is available for the workstation from the
University, then I would say go that route.  Check with your Univ staff,
they are likely some substantial discounts.

 The best of course would be to get both.  A workstation, and a cheap $1500
PC.


