>> There is a bug in the V4.1 SCSI driver that will only allow it to read
>> something like (2 ** 21) or (2 ** 20) LBNs.  After that it wraps
>> around to the beginning of the disk.  It is fixed in V4.2.

>Yet tech support at Western-Scientific says their 2.1 Gb drive can
>function if no partition is greater than 1 meg...

I've had no experience with the company - but their tech support in not
apparently knowledgeable. Blocks are referenced from the start of the
disk - not the start of a partition. Disks don't know from partitions -
that is the magic of an operating system to split it up.

Sure you can buy the disk - but the maximum space on the disk you can
usewill by 1Gb. The reason this exists (someone correct me if I am
wrong) is the difference between the original SCSI 1 command set which
only allowed 20 or 21 bits to be sent in the command to specify the
block, and SCSI 2 commands which had extended blocking added in. (it's a
different scsi command which is sent and was added to the driver).

I'd have to check the FAQ about which version of ULTRIX added the
feature (I think 4.2), but don't bother getting the disk before that
magic version. 

	Ezra





