just when it seemed that they were *finally* getting their act together,
they've (at worst) made an about-face back toward ignominy, or (at best)
started running around in circles, dithering.

Although a decision not to support OSF/1 on their R3000 boxes wouldn't
affect us directly (ditto a decision not to provide an R4000 upgrade),
the lack of credibility it would engender would be a major factor in any
future relationship with Digital.  After all, a lot of people *did* base
their plans on DEC's promises to support OSF/1, and on their promises to
provide R4000 upgrades.

The first "yes we will" / "no we won't" / "yes we will; really" flip-flop
was bad enough.  But the fact that the question has now been reopened is
absolutely astonishing.

We recently issued a PO for a couple of Alpha boxes.  There were people
in our group who expressed strong reservations about doing business with
Digital, for all the obvious reasons.  Nonetheless, DEC did get the sale
this time, because their price/performance ratio was superb, and the demo
unit we had seemed solid and reliable (and the software surprisingly good,
considering that it's pre-release beta stuff).  But if we were ordering
today, the anti-DEC sentiment would unquestionably be a lot stronger.

| If others feel this way, write to your DEC person as well as here.

I've done so.  But I have a nagging fear that DEC is so bloated, and their
bureaucracy so thick, that no matter how helpful and sympathetic our sales
rep may be, complaining to him is unlikely to have much effect.  After all,
he'll have to relay it to his manager, who may or may not relay it to *his*
manager, who probably would have to relay it to yet another person before
it even leaves the Toronto office.  How likely is it that it will ever make
the long journey down to the jokers who are making these decisions?  And if
it did, how likely is it that they'd even listen?  Sigh.

