(Apolgies in advance to those of you who have seen this in other forums 
such as DECUServe.) 

I have a client who has a pair of never-been-used 8" near-gigabyte SMD drives
(made by Northern Telecom) and a QD33 controller. I have told him that he'd be
far better off buying a CMD SCSI controller and some new drives, but he has his
heart set on using what he has. 

We're not having much luck.  When trying to format the drive, it sits there
apparently doing nothing for about 15 minutes, then follows about 1 min 40 
seconds of long-sounding seeks, and then the QD33 reports a timeout  from
the drive.  

In order to attempt to elicit a failure somewhat earlier, we tried telling
the controller to partition the drive into two logical drives, the first one
having only ten cylinders.  In that case the "apparently doing nothing" phase
lasts only a few seconds.  Same symptoms otherwise, except that the ~1:40 of
seeks sound a lot quicker (shorter head movement). 

The NT drives are not among the ones for which parameters are provided in the 
Emulex manual (or the info on their BBS).  The "unknowns" in the setup are:

	- Drive config: should the drive be configured to enable or disable a 
	SECTOR signal simultaneous with the INDEX (once per revolution) signal?

	- Drive config:  It can be configured so that a TAG2 by itself will do 
	a head switch, or so that a headswitch requires a TAG2 and a TAG1.  
	Which way does the QD33 do it?

	- Drive servo clock option:  A switch which "when 'on', routes servo 
	clock through the read clock line when not reading; when reading, read 
	clock is routed through the read clock line. With th eswitch 'off', 
	read clock is always through the read clock line."  Again, we don't
	know which way the QD33 wants it.  

We have tried all eight possible combinations of the above, with no change in
symptoms. 

	- QD33 config, source of index and sector signals:  The QD33 can be 
	optioned to look for index and sector signals on either the A 
	(control) or B (data) cables.  The NT drives provide them on BOTH.  
	We have been trying it on B, as this is what the QD33 manual 
	recommends (it allows the controller to do rotational position sensing
	for request sequence optimization, not that that is a big issue). 

I suppose we should try another eight iterations of the three drive settings
mentioned above, this time with this set to look for the singals on the A 
cable.  

	- QD33 config, "GAP n" parameters:  There are three parameters settable 
	in the NOVRAM called GAP1, GAP2, and GAP3.  These vary for different 
	types of drives in Emulex's table.  I strongly suspect that our 
	problems are due to wrong values for these, esp. since the drive 
	manual calls for a delay after the sector pulse before beginning 
	writing and these values have to do with "recording format for each
	sector.  The recording format allows gaps as, for example, between
	header and data fields."  Alas the Emulex manual does not provide 
	a formula by which to calculate them.  The range of possible values is 
	VERY large so a trial-and-error approach isn't feasible for these. 

I left pretty much this same info as a question on Emulex's BBS, but I don't
expect much -- they're not supporting the controller any longer.  

Any ideas???

	--- Jamie Hanrahan, Kernel Mode Systems, San Diego CA
Internet:  jeh@cmkrnl.com  Uucp: uunet!cmkrnl!jeh  CIS: 74140,2055


