|>overviews of rollout of various software (and lots of other Alpha
|>information can be found on gatekeeper.dec.com:pub/DEC/Alpha/osf1-public-schedule.ps
|>
|>By packaging it separately, it can be released separately from the
|>base system; otherwise 3D support would have to be tied to the base
|>system's release schedule, possibly/probably delaying its availability.
|>And Alpha/OSF makes it much less painful to install device drivers
|>automatically (and a future release will support dynamic loading of device
|>drivers).
 
To me it makes more sense to package the driver with the board than with the
O/S. With a MACH kernel I might want to write my own operating system or
adapt a system like HURD, I would not then want to buy OSF/1 just to
get the device driver.

If I have a rack of alpha PCs doing Monte Carlo stuff the only part of the O/S
I would be using would be the ethernet driver and memory interface. For
that application using MACH could quite possibly repay the development cost
which is all I need to justify an academic research O/S program. Being
able to buy drivers separately may be something that we start asking DEC
to do. After all they have little interest in flogging OSF/1 or W/NT to
us, its only the DEC written drivers they will be making money on.

Another advantage of going that way is you could completely slash the O/S
requirement. No disk handling, no virtual memory, just straight performance.
It would be a one task machine but would be far more cost effective than any
other way of doing it. In additon you don't suffer much of a performance
hit over the speed of a Workstation alpha because GEANT type codes are
pretty cache unfriendly. Just about all that works is the I-cache. 


Phill Hallam-Baker


