In article <149.2b9f20d9@ohstpy.mps.ohio-state.edu>, sidwell@ohstpy.mps.ohio-state.edu writes:

|>I'm interested in the CPU upgrade options for the DecStation 5000/25 (25mhz
|>personal decstation), and can find nothing in the catalogs. I'm willing to
|>consider either the Alpha or R4000 route. Is there any info at
|>gatekeeper.dec.com I should be aware of? Currently I have 40 machines, and
|>may upgrade a substantial number if things look promising. I also have 5
|>DS5000/240 and 28 DS5000/200 (approximately- I begin to lose count). We're
|>running a (very) compute intensive process with these machines organized
|>into 3 compute farms, and have more than 2 years to go on the project. 
|>
|>I am aware that an Alpha migration path exists for the DS5000/240. This
|>only covers 5 of our machines of course, and means moving to a new
|>operating system (maybe a good idea down the road, tho). Upgrading the model
|>200s looks uneconomical. 
|>
|>We have several libraries for which we don't have easy access to the source
|>code (CERNLIB, LUND, etc for high energy physics) which are not scheduled to be
|>ported to the Alpha yet (someday). Is there a binary translator for
|>the Alpha from MIPs code? 

CERNLIB is running on ALpha under both OSF/1 and VMS. The `Official' CERN
port only exists for VMS at the moment. The OSF/1 port is trickier since there
is the 32bit/64bit question to be resolved. The code does work though if you
turn on enough flags and compile from source. To release an `official' version
I hope that CERN are going to fix the system for 64 bits.

If you have a license to run CERNLIB you must also have access to the source
code. You should contact CERN for details. LUND is maintained by CERN so
they are the contact point for that as well. In fact since you are Ohio state
you only need to go to the ZEUS group and you can get binary copies of
most of CERNLIB since Ohio is in ZEUS and is thus a CERN collaborating
institute. If we are talking about the ZEUS DECstations then the port for
ZEUS is already done for OSF/1 by the Bologna and for VMS by DESY and RAL.


|>My understanding is that the R4000 is binary compatible with the R3000 
|>family. Is that true? 
|>
|>We are currently running Ultrix 4.2A. Would we need to upgrade if migrating 
|>to the R4000?

In order to get the performance advantage you have to recompile. Otherwise your
speedup will be limited to the clock rate increase. Since you are running 
CERNLIB  you may not see a lot of speedup from a clock upgrade alone since
GEANT and all BOS or ZEBRA based codes in general tend to thrash CPU
caches. Unless you speed up your memory you may be disappointed. 


Phill Hallam-Baker


