Fred,

The DEC 3000 Model 400 has two memory option slots (banks) that can hold
either 16, 32 or 64MB options. The model 500 has 4 slots.  Thus, if you 
purchase a system with 48MB, both option slots are filled (1 32MB module 
and 1 16MB module).

The 16MB option was developed to allow customers who needed more than 32MB,
but not 64MB, to buy a cost-effective option to solve their problem.  However,
if you will ever need to have more than 48MB, you should seriously consider
buying the base system with a 64MB option in one slot with the other slot left
open.

Bottom line, and as you suspected, to expand a pre-configured 48MB system you will need to remove the low-density (16MB) module.  Trade-in values and business practices on used memory vary by country.  Talk to your local Digital account rep on how to best handle the extra 16MB module.
  
-- 
