Using MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE without Xdm is not useless, it's just limited.  You'd
only want to do it if only one user was going to login in and use X.
Hardwiring an authorization in this way is useful because many people have a
"home" workstation that they don't want anybody else to log in on the console
or display X to.

If you want to allow many users to log in and use X with per-user 
display authorization then you need something like Xdm to generate and install 
a new security token (in the user's home directory and on the server).
Xprompter just doesn't know how to do it.

Xdm (with MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE enabled) is what is used on DEC OSF/1.

