In article <1nkt85INNgfu@coral.bucknell.edu>, sloane@bucknell.edu (Thomas Sloane) writes:
|>      From local vms machine, what is the vms equivalent
|> to %xhost + ?

when a session is up and running, you would use the session manager
to customize the security 'database'.  Select the Options.Security
from the session manager menu bar.  Then enter * * * for the
usename, machine, and transport.

If there is no session, and you still want to be able to display
on the server, there are several things that you need to do.

You will need to bring up the machine with the sysgen parameter
WINDOW_SYSTEM = 0, then start DECwindows manually (or in the
system manager's startup file) with @sys$manager:decw$startup server
(although it might be @decw$startup "" server, i'm not entirely sure
it's been a while since i've needed to do this.  

You do the above because any window that is displayed on the
server will be realized BEHIND the login box, a rather nasty
annoyance if you want to look at it.

You will also need to create a file in your sys$manager account
called (I think) decw$server_hosts_trusted.dat with the contents of

* * *

the first is the user, the second is the nodename, the third is the
transport.  This is actually documented somewhere in the DECwindows
books that come with the system.  I may also be somewhere in the
comments of some of the decwindows startup files in sys$manger.

okee dokee?

