|> Ug!
|> 
|>      Me needum core.  Needum core for heap big PDP-11/40!  Make run!  
|> Well, anyways, barbaric as my header may sound, I need core-mem for a 
|> PDP-11/40.  Or probably any Unibus PDP. :)  I just got a PDP-11/40 in
|> pieces and I was gonna get it up and running and test it out with the
|> memory in my 11/34a until I could get it it's own memory but the only
|> module I have in my 11/34a is 256K which is I think about 10 times what
|> the 11/40 is capable of handling.  So if you have some (Preferrably in
|> CA, USA) that you wouldn't mind parting with, I'd be interested in it.
|> 
|> 							-Scott

Depending on its configuration, your 11/40 should be able to handle
just as much memory as the 11/34. Check if there's a module in the CPU
backplane right beside the combined bus-jumper/terminator that connects
the CPU backplane to the next one along. If there is a hex module in this
slot, then your 11/40 has a KT11-D memory management unit, and can address
up to quarter of a meg. If not, you're limited to 64k. (DEC spares
companies sell the KT11-D second-hand for about 75 pounds over here,
with the KJ11 stack limit being about another 25.)

The 11/34 memory (if it's a single module) may well be designed for 
a Modified Unibus Device slot, in which case you'll want a DD11-C or
similar backplane to put it in. 

James
