In article <1993Mar11.194310.27912@cs.cornell.edu>, chapman@cs.cornell.edu (Richard Chapman) writes...
>jlothian@castle.ed.ac.uk (J Lothian) writes:
> 
>I have a pdp-11/20, into which I want to put several more peripherals,
>(have a KL-11 and diode ROM, want to add PC11, RX02) but there are
>only two expansion slots (SPC) at the rear of the 11/20 backplane with
>the cpu in it. I have a spare 9-slot expansion backplane used in an
>11/34 (you jumpered it in behind the backplane with the cpu on the
>11/34) but I am not sure whether I can use it or not. The obvious
>question is what to do with the molex connectors on the expansion
>backplane, since the 11/20 supplies power via card-edge connectors
>that plug into the top the backplane. What else needs to be done? Can
>anyone address the differences in the unibus implementations on the
>two machines? Thanks,

	The only thing you have to do is convert your molex to match the
	power connections. (The newer 9-slot backplanes will work fine
	with any of the older unibus stuff.)

	Were I faced with this problem I would probably take a mating
	molex connector and wire it to accept the molex connector from
	the 9-slot backplane.

	Also, you can fake out some units (like bus repeaters) by connecting
	the ACLO and DCLO signals. Best to only connect the +5 and + and -
	15 volt signals.

