-
-                              Ultrix Load Spikes,
-                                Network Delays
-                                      &
-                              Best of all: Fixes
-
-                     Corey Satten, corey@cac.washington.edu
-                       Networks and Distributed Computing
-                            University of Washington
-                               Seattle, Washington
-                                     3/8/93
-
-Summary:
-
-    Four unrelated Ultrix kernel bugs are presented along with fixes.
-    They are bundled together mostly for my convenience in posting.
-    This contains information I promised to post at the Q&A after
-    Jonathan Kay and Joseph Pasquale's paper at San Diego 1/93 Usenix.
-
-    Bug 1: bogus select collisions cause load spikes.
-    Bug 2: select is broken on ptys causing programs to burn CPU cycles.
-    Bug 3: TCP delayed ACKs are not being sent properly causing retransmissions.
-    Bug 4: TCP retransmission delays may grow very large.
-
-Detail:
-
-  Bug 1:
-
-    On 9/30/91 I posted an article discussing select collisions and
-    provided programs which cause them to happen improperly on Ultrix.
-    Bogus select collisions came to my attention because they were so
-    common on our systems that they made the load average a useless metric
-    and caused occasional problems with programs which alter their behavior
-    based upon load average (such as sendmail).  That former posting is
-    included for reference as "old_post.shar" (because I have learned from
-    experience that I will be flooded with requests for it anyway).
-
-    Credit for the fix is due Nancy Johnson Burr in our CS department.
-    The fix is to file fifo_gnodeops.c.
-
-  Bug 2:
-
-    While investigating the occasional runaway xterm process, I discovered
-    that pasting into a program which doesn't read the data in a timely
-    fashion can cause the xterm to burn CPU cycles on Ultrix.  To witness
-    this, try pasting several hundred bytes into sleep 1000 and watch your
-    xterm rack up cycles.  Although this sounds like a perverse thing to
-    do, remember that one need only accidently hit the wrong mouse button.
-    Furthermore, even when the paste is intentional, if the program receiving
-    the data is slow enough that the xterm gets a couple hundred bytes
-    ahead, the bug can cause the xterm to loop trying to paste and thus
-    compete with the already slow receiver.
-
-    To fix this, I adapted the 4.3BSD code for the offending case.
-    The fix is to the file tty_pty.c.
-
-  Bug 3:
-
-    Five times per second, a timer in the kernel causes Ultrix to look
-    through the list of established TCP connections for those which need
-    to have a delayed ack sent.  The proper action is to handle all the
-    connections but according to a comment, someone at DEC changed this
-    to only ack the first one in the list, figuring the next ones would
-    probably get ack'd on the next pass.  If there are sufficiently few
-    TCP connections or they are mostly not needing delayed acks, this may
-    be true, however on a busy system, it can happen that delayed ack
-    processing never gets to the connections at the end of the list (the
-    older connections).  We had users complaining that TCP connections
-    would start out OK but get worse and worse.  On one system, reportedly
-    the connections became unusable after as little as 20 minutes.  I
-    discovered that a certain "expect script" I have which communicates
-    over an rsh would have the unusual property that only the most recently
-    started instance would run at normal speed, starting a new one would
-    slow down the older ones and killing the newer ones would speed them
-    back up in reverse order.
-
-    This bug interacts in a particularly unfortunate way with bug #4.
-
-    Again, the solution was to compare 4.3BSD to Ultrix to see what DEC
-    broke.  Credit is due my associate, Martin Hunt for doing the work.
-    This fix is to file tcp_timer.c.
-
-  Bug 4:
-
-    Under some conditions, the TCP code increases (properly) the delay
-    before attempting to retransmit.  This is part of the adaptive strategy
-    to avoid unnecessary retransmissions.  Unfortunately, there is a bug
-    in this code (possibly originating at Berkeley since it seems that
-    some old versions of NCD software may also have it) which can cause
-    this timeout to grow when retransmissions are needed but never to
-    shrink again later.  This, interacts most unfortunately with bug #3
-    and can cause the consequences of not sending a delayed ack to be a
-    delay of many tens of seconds before retrying.  Of course, acks are
-    also piggy-backed on outbound traffic, so this bug was often noticed
-    as output being one keystroke behind what it should have been.
-
-    I am told DEC partially fixed this in Ultrix 4.2a but a better fix
-    (below) was provided to comp.protocols.tcp-ip and comp.windows.x last
-    year by Paul Barton-Davis (pauld@cs.washington.edu) and Dan Lanciani
-    (Harvard).  This fix is to file tcp_input.c.  I have not tested this
-    code because it was accidently left out of our last round of kernel
-    builds and it's not important enough to us to force another round of
-    new kernels and reboots.  It will probably go in next time but we seem
-    to be running pretty well now even without it.  This is what Paul said:
-
-        OK, so here's a better patch [than in Ultrix 4.2a].
-        This one gets as close as possible to the "right" answer
-        that one could get without a timer for each outstanding
-        packet, which is overkill.  Its not a diff, but hopefully
-        Dave or someone else will get the idea.  A key point to
-        notice is that rxtshift is reset at a point whther ti_ack
-        has already been found to be greater than snd_una.  This is
-        a key difference to the simplistic approach that DEC took
-        in the Ultrix 4.2 code.  Again, thanks to Dan Lanciani for
-        this.
-
-        ----piece of tcp_input------
-
-
-                        } else {
-                                /* 
-                                 * No rtt measurement yet - use the
-                                 * unsmoothed rtt.  Set the variance
-                                 * to half the rtt (so our first
-                                 * retransmit happens at 2*rtt)
-                                 */
-                                tp->t_srtt = tp->t_rtt << 3;
-                                tp->t_rttvar = tp->t_rtt << 1;
-                        }
-                        tp->t_rtt = 0;
-        #ifndef ORIG
-                }
-                if (tp->t_timer[TCPT_PERSIST] == 0)
-        #endif
-                        tp->t_rxtshift = 0;
-                        TCPT_RANGESET(tp->t_rxtcur, 
-                              (((tp->t_srtt >> 2) + tp->t_rttvar) >> 1) + 1,
-                              TCPTV_MIN, TCPTV_REXMTMAX);
-        #ifdef  ORIG
-                }
-        #endif
-
-                /*
-                 * If all outstanding data is acked, stop retransmit
-                 * timer and remember to restart (more output or persist).
-                 * If there is more data to be acked, restart retransmit
-                 * timer, using current (possibly backed-off) value.
-                 */
-                if (ti->ti_ack == tp->snd_max) {
-                        tp->t_timer[TCPT_REXMT] = 0;
-                        needoutput = 1;
-                } else if (tp->t_timer[TCPT_PERSIST] == 0)
-                        tp->t_timer[TCPT_REXMT] = tp->t_rxtcur;
-
-
-DISCLAIMER:
-
-On our systems, the fixes presented below were applied on top of various
-other changes.  I believe I accurately backed out these other changes
-before producing the enclosed diffs against vanilla Ultrix 4.2a, however
-please remember that the burden of validating the code I'm providing
-remains on you, especially for fix #4 which even I haven't tried.
-
-DONT ASK FOR BINARIES
