In article <1nnj0gINNd3p@srvr1.engin.umich.edu>, hillig@U.Chem.LSA.UMich.EDU (Kurt Hillig) writes:
>
>On both machines, when booting, the console messages read in part:
>
> [ COnfiguration deleted. ]
>	pid 6 was killed in exec: error reading data area
>
>after which the system continues to boot - apparently normally.
>However, I find that routed doesn't work any more!

Check the error log.  The error message is actually pretty
clear about what happened.  There was an error while the
exec(2) system call was trying to read in some program's
data area.  Odds are it was an I/O error.  Since routed
is suspect, use sum(1) to check that it isn't broken.
The expected checksum can be found in the inventory file
in /usr/etc/subsets/*.inv.  It is probably in the INET
subset.

You might also have other broken programs.  Running fverify(8)
for the important subsets might be worthwhile.  Running rzdisk(8)
to do a bad block scan might also be good.  Read the fverify(8)
manual page carefully before running it.  It likes to fix things
(according to how IT things they should be fixed).  Quite often
this is not what you really want.

>
> [ Commentary on routed. ]

In looking one of my V4.3 systems, pid #6 has got to be something really
early in the start-up.  I'm running shadowing and start up shaderrd before
the initial fsck(8) in /etc/rc runs.  It's pid is 14.  
