Dave Sill (de5@ORNL.GOV) wrote:
> We've got people with DAT drives of various manufacture on systems of various
> manufacture.
> 
> What kind of tapes do we need to stock?  Does one "size" fit all? 
> 
Not really -  the 1.2 GByte older drives use a 60-Meter tape, and most
newer 2-to-8 GByte drives (compressed or not) use a 90-Meter tape.

Many newer drives will also read/write the older 60-Meter tapes, but may not
be able to use them in compression mode (depending on the design).

If you absolutely, positively have to stock only one type, buy 60-Meter.


> Since all DAT is digital, is there a difference between tapes used for DDS or
> audio?
> 
There is a *definite* difference between Audio and DDS tapes!  The cassette
shell of a DDS tape is manufactured to much tighter dimensional standards.
Some cheaper Audio tapes can actually jam and tear in a DDS drive.

DDS tape itself is more rugged, since it is designed to withstand the
repositioning action needed when the drive tries to re-read data which needs
correction.  Audio-grade tapes are designed to stream only, and thus are
more susceptible to failure under this condition.

The magnetic media formulation is, I believe, different from both 60-Meter
and 90-Meter DDS.   The data dropout rate when using Audio tapes tends to be
significantly higher.    Please be aware that if you use an Audio tape,
all bets for data reliability are off!


> Are all DAT tapes preformatted?
> 
Gee, I feel stupid -  I don't know the answer to this!  I suspect not,
though.


> Are there multiple densities of DDS?  Seems like they used to advertized
> as 1GB, but are now advertised as 2GB.
> 
There may be a difference, although the relative lengths of the tape
cassettes seem to indicate not.   NOTE that 60-Meter and 90-Meter tapes
use a *different* magnetic formulation!    Also, the older 1 GByte drives
cannot properly use 90-Meter tapes because the tension through the tape
path is different from 60-Meter.   I'm not sure just what the actual
helical-path bit density is, but I do know that it is *very* high!


> Do drives with compression use the same tape?  Are there any makes/models that
> you recommend buying or avoiding?
> 
Many drives with compression must use the 90-Meter tapes, but they can use
either 60-Meter or 90-Meter tapes in non-compression mode.  I am highly
prejudiced of course, but I recommend HP simply because it seems to work
fine on my workstation at the office as well as my PC at home.


> Please respond by e-mail.  I'll summarize responses.
> 
I tried, but something went wrong at this end, so I thought I'd post.
I hope this helps.

Chuck.

