Thanks for pointing this out... I forgot.   I also neglected to mention
that there is more than one kind of hardware compression available these
days for DDS drives.

The two that come to mind are STAC (available from WangDAT among others),
and the much more common DCLZ (used by HP among others).

Data written in compressed mode using STAC cannot be read by DCLZ drives,
and vice-versa.  I'm not sure if anyone produces a drive that can do both.
I'm also aware of add-on boards which are inserted in the SCSI bus of
non-compression drives, but these likely produce yet another format with
respect to header information.

My understanding is that the combination of DDS and DCLZ is pretty well
the accepted compression standard these days.

Chuck.

