| local radio shop (Kenwood HiFi Center, Falkoner Alle), however they
| were 120 minutes (still not meters), and cost 69 dKr a piece (approx
| 10.5 US$).

Are you *sure* they were audio tapes?  If so, somebody in a sales
office is acting on their own, since officially they aren't supported.
(The drive vendors say they can shed more and gum up the heads; they
also aren't supposed to be as rigid or as tight mechanical tolerances,
which increases the risk of jamming.)  The audio tapes definitely
aren't good for as many reuses.

| We have never had any read errors on these tapes.

You've been lucky so far, or BASF's volumes are low enough that they
actually use the same tape and shell for both audio and data grade
(it has been rumored that several vendors have done this, although
I've never been able to confirm it).

| I don't think you can retension a DAT tape. If you issue the mt retension
| command, it returns immediately. I think this command is only relevant for
| the 1/4" tapes, as they have the tape driven by a rubber band.
| 

Yep.  Ardat, at least, says retension is completely unnecessary,
and so they make the comman a NOP.
