In article <1993Feb26.120245.1@ntrs.uucp>, dsm1@ntrs.uucp writes:
|>
|>Hmmmmm...  This stuff is interesting to me.   We have VXT2000's (running -gulp-
|>version 1.0 VXT software, I believe) that we use to conduct Xsessions over
|>LAT to VMS machines and over IP to ULTRIX 4.2a machine.
|>
|>I have noticed that whenever I end a VMS Xsession, it resets the VXT server,
|>but you all are mentioning that that also happens when you end an ULTRIX
|>Xsession.  This does not happen to me.  I just close all my windows and 
|>that's the end.
|>
|>	o	Can you explain to me what 'The end of an Xsession' means
|>		under ULTRIX?  What process is special?

I should have been more explicit - IP X session support under vxt is managed
via the XDMCP protocal (implemented by.. ta dah.. xdm). Usually what happens
with xdm is that some process is left in the foreground by 
/usr/lib/xdm/Xsession or ~/.xsession script (the way Digital suggests to set 
up IP X sessions on ULTRIX makes dxsession the foreground application, - I 
have X.desktop set up as my foreground application in a local .xsession file).

When this process dies, XDMCP is supposed to be responsible for cleaning up the
X server for the next session - things like killing off other clients that are 
displaying on the X terminal. Clean slate, ready for the next user to
log onto the terminal.

This usually involves a X server reset being generated somewhere (I don't know
the specifics about whether the client or the server is the culprit).

dxsession is also the "special process" if you're running from a ULTRIX 
workstation (unless you're running xdm like me). The mechanics are a bit 
different to kill off a session (since XDMCP doesn't come into play here)
but the result is the same. In the case of a workstation, dxsession is called
by a program called Xprompter (Digital's equivalent of xdm for a locally
attached graphics head). I don't know for sure if dxsession/Xprompter
generates a signal directly to the local X server to kill off the session.

I plead ignorance on LAT X sessions, so I don't know what VMS does when
a session gets killed.

|>
|>	o	Also, why is xdm not sending a reset-server command?
|>

xdm thought the vxt terminal was being managed via XDMCP.
Because XDMCP support is .. uh .. incomplete in releases of VXT software 
before 1.2. xdm thought the server would clean up at the end of a session, 
and the VXT didn't do it.

