In article <ZHAO.93Mar1085350@pylos.nmsu.edu>, zhao@nmsu.edu (Z. Zhao) writes:
>
>From the quotes I received, I was told there are 1.6GB hard disks in
>my dec5000/240, RZ56 1GB with tape box + RZ57 600MB with cdrom box.
>But, from booting and 'df' I can see 1GB showing up only. I am using 
>ultrix4.2. Is there any instruction in ultrix to tell me what is my
>system?

This probably will end up being a summary of some of the answer
you're seen otherwise.  First though, a minor detail.  The RZ56
is the 600 MB disk and the RZ57 is the 1 GB disk.

Using the console monitor you can check the system configuration
with the command "cnfg".  By itself this will give a summary of
what the different TURBOchannel adapter are, what CPU and how
much memory you have, and probably a little commentary on the
graphics adapter if you have a DECstation.  One of the things
this will tell you is which TURBOchannel adapters are SCSI
adapters.  With that you can query each adapter to find out
what it has on it; "cnfg #".

When ULTRIX boots, it prints a device listing.  This information
is also saved in the error log and can be reviewed using the
uerf(8) command.  The boot messages are record type 300.  For
looking at the boot message you also probably want to read them
in reverse chronological order:

# uerf -R -r 300

There are programs that will tell you about specific devices; file(1)
being the easiest.  The problem is that if a device doesn't have a
special device file associated with it, then you can't use file(1)
to find out what it is.  For network devices netstat(1) should be
able to find all of them it can know about.

Finally, you have the general problem that you can have devices on
your system that ULTRIX doesn't know to look for.  The console monitor
should be able to find these devices using cnfg, but unless the kernel
has been configured to find them, it won't.

The data structures that ULTRIX uses to keep track of what devices
it has are lists within the kernel.  Tracking through these lists
isn't exceptionally hard and there are programs that will do it.
For example, Monitor has an option "names", that will go through
these devices lists and print what it can find on the system.  What
it doesn't do is attach detailed device types to device names.

Monitor is available from: gatekeeper.dec.com:/pub/DEC/monitor.tar.Z
